<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 204 0 R
/T (John sends an email to his colleague, Mark, and includes their manager in the CC field. The manager will see that Mark received the email as well as the fact that it was sent by John. True or False?)
/E (John sends an email to his colleague, Mark, and includes their manager in the CC field. The manager will see that Mark received the email as well as the fact that it was sent by John. True or False?)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
287 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 205 0 R
/T (True. When someone is included in the CC field, all recipients of the email can see the email addresses in the CC field. So, if the manager is included in the CC field, Mark will see that the manager received the email as well as that it was sent by John.)
/E (True. When someone is included in the CC field, all recipients of the email can see the email addresses in the CC field. So, if the manager is included in the CC field, Mark will see that the manager received the email as well as that it was sent by John.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
288 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 206 0 R
/T (John sends an email to his colleague, Mark, and includes their manager in the BCC field. The manager will see that Mark received the email, but Mark will not be aware that the manager received it as well. True or False?)
/E (John sends an email to his colleague, Mark, and includes their manager in the BCC field. The manager will see that Mark received the email, but Mark will not be aware that the manager received it as well. True or False?)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
289 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 207 0 R
/T (True. When you include someone in the BCC \(Blind Carbon Copy\) field, the BCC recipient \(in this case, the manager\) receives the email, but their email address is hidden from all other recipients. Mark would see that he received the email but would not know that the manager was also a recipient.)
/E (True. When you include someone in the BCC \(Blind Carbon Copy\) field, the BCC recipient \(in this case, the manager\) receives the email, but their email address is hidden from all other recipients. Mark would see that he received the email but would not know that the manager was also a recipient.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
